 masters PhD University of Michigan she's  been at Hillsdale since 1989 she has won  the Emily Daughtry award she's the  faculty advisor to the lamplighters into  Sigma Pi and here's what she says about  the student she's very eloquent on the  subject of the students they're  hard-working trustworthy honest  respectful and collegial and collegial  means she goes on to say serious  partnership between student and teacher  everyone bringing something to the table  and everyone taking as much or more away  she says she counts at her chief  blessing to be a part of the lives of  for her students she's a teacher in the  full sense  I will tell you that she's a very  expressive woman and her expressions  heavily involve her hands and we'll see  in the tape if she waves her hands  around but if she doesn't it will be  unnatural and and her closest students  will probably have trouble understanding  her because it's like watching sim  afford go on and I miss seeing that  right now she doesn't do it around me  very much but around you she really just  stick you lates Lee Baron professor of  the year and God bless you all  Thank You dr. Arnn for the kind  introduction and welcome to all of you  who are joining us today imagine  yourself at a lively gathering around a  warming fire in a toga in Greece in  approximately 400 BC have you ever  wondered what it would be like to be  part of those conversations to ponder  with the ancients questions such as what  is the universe and the basic building  blocks therein or what is matter as a  chemist I am particularly fascinated  with helis ipis and his student  Democritus formulated their ideas that  matter was composed of atoms around 400  BC atoms of small indestructible spheres  of various sizes and shapes obviously  this is not how we understand atoms  today but it was an idea that survived  for centuries it boggles my mind or to  have been there when Plato proposed his  job geometric forms to represent the  elements of the universe to have been  part of the conversation when Aristotle  who was known as a key observer over the  world proposed his four earthly elements  fire earth air water and a four as well  as the fifth element the ether because  heavenly bodies were not made of earthly  material he's ancient Greek philosophers  not only pondered what is matter but why  does it matter they wrestled with such  questions as if we exist in this world  and this is the way it is how hot we  live within it what does it mean to be a  human being of character what makes the  good life they were trying to discern  the composition of the universe through  direct observation and logical  deductions while seemed simultaneously  trying to answer fundamental questions  about being human  in this world and discovering their  place within it these ideas about the  natural world did not develop in a  vacuum but through interaction  conversation and within a framework of  being a person of virtue and character  these philosophers were not only asking  what is this world within which we live  but also why does it matter  their ideas were foundational to the way  we do science today as they influenced  philosopher scientists such as the cart  and bacon although ultimately decart's  rationalism and Bacon's empiricism  became two different approaches to  exploring the natural world they both  were still pondering the same things as  the Greeks what is matter and why does  it matter for them these questions were  not separate but rather part of a  cohesive whole as they were men of both  reason and faith they did not view their  exploration of the natural world and  their faith as two opposing approaches  to searching for truth but rather  perspectives that actually work together  to enhance our understanding of the  universe in a synergistic way they were  interested in ways to investigate the  world around them as philosopher  scientists while simultaneously viewing  their findings within the framework of  th